Apologies for the length of the post.  I'm just really excited because
(1) this was my first game getting past the Quest (hurray for lava +
collapsing drawbridges).
(2) I ascended with AC -16
(3) I didn't have reflection for most of the game.

When I started this game I resolved to try not to take unnecessary
risks (which definitely did not happen).  So I dipped for Excalibur at
xplevel 5, and when I reached dlvl 4 I found an armor shop containing
cloak of invisibility, cloak of displacement, oilskin cloak, and
jumping boots.  I donned the displacement cloak and the boots, having
already made myself invisible (wand).  I went into the Mines and found
a bag of holding.  When I got to Minetown the altar was coaligned and
there was a delicatessen with two iceboxes directly across from it --
I jumped for joy and sacrificed, but nothing good came out of it.  In
Izchak's shop there were four candles and a magic lamp.  I lit the
lamp, descended further, and left with the luckstone.

Then I did Sokoban -- the top level had the bag of holding, so I
didn't bother with it, but I did set up my stash on the bottom level
(I had to lug a box about four dungeon levels to do this).  The level
below Sokoban was the rather scary Big Room -- I survived it (barely)
by staying near the stairs with my awesome ring of regeneration.  A
level below that was a coaligned temple, where I pulled out my three
wands of create monster and sacrificed, hoping for and receiving
Mjollnir.  I never really used it because I never found gauntlets of
power and preferred to wield Excalibur instead.  Later on I sacrificed
more and obtained Ogresmasher and Grayswandir, none of which I really
used either.

By the time I reached the Quest level and was xp14 I wanted to
postpone the Quest until I got fire resistance, better AC (was at
~-5), and a tinning kit.  So I went down to Medusa (blindfold +
mirror) and got Perseus' shield of reflection, which I lost about ten
minutes later to an unidentified scroll of destroy armor (yes, I was
being impatient and trying to identify scrolls by reading them, shame
on me).

Having explored all the rooms before Medusa and not found a tinning
kit, I decided to enter the Quest anyway (I figured I'd take the risk
with lack of fire resistance -- which I'd get anyway through eating
the monsters -- and bad AC since I'd heard Surtur was easy to
defeat).  There I discovered I actually *did* have fire resistance,
and remembered that I'd been crowned.  On my first trip down I wore my
ring of regeneration (for hunger) and tried to kill only a small
number of giants so I wouldn't waste giant corpses and could kill the
rest when I was hungry again; after killing Surtur I sporadically
returned to the Quest until my strength was 18/**.

I then tried to descend past Medusa (around this time something
happened to my cloak of displacement so I switched to oilskin), but
this monster with a wand of death scared the living shit out of me, so
I rubbed my lamp and got GDSM (which reduced my AC to about -11
without a shield).  With GDSM I cleared out the castle pretty easily
and somewhere around this time I genocided arch-liches and started
twoweaponing Excalibur with a silver saber.  I wished for two blessed
scrolls of charging and saved the other charges for later.

I descended into Gehennom (without reflection) and came face-to-face
with a retreating master mind flayer.  (This was my second stupid,
unnecessary risk of the game.)  Rather than leaving it alone I was
determined to kill it and eat its brain.  He stole about four points
of my INT and half my memory.  The wand of wishing became an
unidentified copper wand, meaning I spent about an hour frantically
searching for my wand in my stash, Vlad's Tower, etc.  When I finally
identified the copper wand I discovered it was in my bag of holding at
(0:0) -- but by then I'd lost my (de-identified) scrolls of charging,
probably to fire traps, which meant I spent an inordinate amount of
time hanging around Gehennom scouring the area for unidentified
scrolls and/or a magic marker/magic lamp.  Around this time I found
speed boots and lost my oilskin cloak (yes, to another de-identified
scroll of destroy armor...should've known to be careful).  At least I
got to genocide master mind flayers.

Despite my pathetic AC (-16) and lack of reflection (which, since I
had all the other resistances, produced no ill effects except for
allowing lightning to destroy three rings of regeneration and an
amulet of life saving), the demon princes whom I didn't bribe were
dispatched pretty easily with a few blows from my twoweaponed +5
Excalibur and +1 silver saber.  Around this time I found an athame,
which didn't help too much except when being healed by nurses.  After
finding the vibrating square and fake Wizard's tower, I decided I was
sick of searching for scrolls and wrested the last charge from the
(0:0) wand of wishing, which I used on 7 candles.  I wand of death'd
the Wizard and defeated the priests in the Sanctum pretty easily by
retreating into a corridor.

I got the Amulet and started my trek upwards, leaving the invocation
artifacts in Orcus-town.  Rodney reappeared in the Castle as I was
levitating over the trapdoors.  Being too lazy to take out my wand of
death, I tried to beat him to death -- bad idea.  He stole the Amulet
and I killed him over a trapdoor, but the amulet wasn't on his person,
meaning I had to scour every inch of VotD for it.  Luckily the Wizard
reappeared carrying the amulet named REAL -- I never risked killing
him by a non-instadeath method again.

I went up to my stash in Sokoban and prepared by taking out 24 C-
rations (for use against Famine) and all the potions of healing I
could find.  The only scrolls of gold detection I had were uncursed
and I'd lost my only potion of unholy water, so I ascended to the
Planes and found all the portals the long hard way.  Luckily they were
pretty easy to find except for the one on the Plane of Air -- I had to
kill practically all the air elementals (which took off ~20 hp apiece,
so I had to use one of my precious potions of full healing) before
finding the portal.

I arrived on the Astral Plane, visited the middle altar first.  It was
guarded by Famine, which I assumed would make me very hungry, but it
merely stunned me instead.  The altar was neutral.  Next I went to the
left altar, guarded by Death.  I had to use another potion of full
healing since the priests kept surrounding me.  Death took off some HP
but I managed to kill him, head for the altar, talk to the priest, and
#offer the Amulet...

